Why those four lines, and not a copied boilerplate:

  * The port itself -- the scripts, the UWP entry point, the Mesa stubs, the
    documentation -- is new work and is published under this MIT license.

  * patches/smash4pc-xbox.patch is a derivative work of Smash4PC, whose LICENSE
    reads "MIT License / Copyright (c) 2026 Petexy / Linexy". MIT allows
    modified versions as long as its notice travels with them, which is what
    the second line above does. Smash4PC's repository is maintained by JosiahG;
    the copyright holder named in its LICENSE file is Petexy / Linexy, and that
    is the name a license file has to carry.

  * patches/mesa-dozen-xbox.patch is a derivative work of Mesa. Every file it
    touches carries an MIT header: Microsoft's for the Dozen driver, Intel's
    for the Vulkan WSI and runtime. Mesa as a whole is a collection of
    components with different licenses -- these particular ones are MIT.

NOTICE.md says what each piece is and what this repository does NOT distribute.
